NOTES={1370: 'Title: Understanding the Perception and Localization. Printed Lecture Number 44 and footer 1.', 1371: 'Learning objective only; no new substantive content.', 1372: 'Contents roadmap only; no new derivation.', 1373: 'Perception extracts environmental information; localization estimates pose in a reference map; mapping constructs an environmental representation. The diagram legend shows dashed blue LiDAR rays, green camera field of view, a red ToF depth sector, gray obstacles, red landmark dots, and pose (x, y, θ). Planning statements describe this pipeline rather than every possible planning formulation.', 1374: 'Historical list: dead reckoning uses speed, direction, and time; resection intersects landmark bearings; celestial navigation uses star elevations and tables; Harrison’s chronometer supports longitude; GPS is associated with a 1995 milestone and indoor/urban obstruction; probabilistic methods include Bayes and Kalman filtering, with 1960 named. The resection diagram shows bearing intersections. Dates are retained as source wording, not independent chronology research.', 1375: 'Five uncertainty categories: changing environment; noisy or limited sensors; actuator noise and wear; approximate models; algorithmic tradeoffs. The comparison with deterministic industrial robots is an idealization, not an absence of physical uncertainty.', 1376: 'Perception turns raw sensor measurements into a useful description of the environment. This repeats the p1373 definition and is linked without padding.', 1377: 'Five-stage pipeline: acquisition, filtering, segmentation, feature extraction, interpretation. The example starts with 360 ranges, discards four zero-valued bad returns, and retains 356. A gap greater than 20 cm splits three clusters. Line fits produce three wall segments; two long parallel walls and one short wall are interpreted as a corridor. That interpretation is illustrative, not a uniqueness proof.', 1378: 'Pulsed LiDAR time of flight: d = ct/2, c = 3 × 10^8 m/s, round-trip time 40 ns, distance 6 m. Each orange point is a range-angle measurement. Approximate RPLidar values are 5.5 Hz, 360 points per revolution, and 12 m maximum range; no model is named. These are illustrative, not universal product specifications. Some RPLIDAR models use triangulation rather than direct pulsed time of flight.', 1379: 'Encoder example: wheel radius 0.05 m, circumference about 0.314 m, 1000 pulses per revolution, about 0.314 mm per pulse, and 620 pulses giving about 0.195 m. Travel assumes calibration and no slip. A gyro reading 30 degrees per second for 2 seconds gives a 60-degree change, not absolute heading without an initial reference. Accelerometer gravity-based tilt interpretation needs a near-static or dynamic-acceleration qualification.', 1380: 'Ten wall ranges: 1.98, 2.03, 2.01, 1.97, 2.02, 2.00, 1.99, 2.04, 1.96, 2.01 m. Mean is 2.001 m and population-convention standard deviation is about 0.02468 m. The slide rounds these to 2.00 m and 0.025 m. Distinguish population and sample conventions, standard deviation and hard bounds, and Gaussian mean from a true value unless unbiasedness is assumed. Actual printed footer is 9, not physical-offset value 11.', 1381: 'Position tracking starts from an approximately known pose. Global localization starts with a known map but an unknown initial pose. The target illustration is decorative, not a calibrated uncertainty plot.', 1382: 'Belief is a conditional probability distribution over planar pose (x, y, θ), recursively informed by control and noisy observations. The two-dimensional Gaussian position plot labels sigma 20. Its negative vertical baseline supports projected contours and does not represent negative probability.', 1383: 'State may include pose, velocity, objects, and landmarks. Transition and measurement models are distinct. Graph arrows are x_(t−1) to x_t, u_t to x_t, and x_t to z_t. The posterior conditions on all available histories; the bottom predicted belief omits z_t despite reusing unbarred bel typography.', 1384: 'A complete state screens off earlier history conditional on the relevant state and control. Both conditional-independence equations were inspected. Current or future controls are not made irrelevant by the Markov assumption.', 1385: 'Bayes prediction integrates the motion model against the previous belief; correction multiplies by likelihood and normalizes. The no-assumption claim cannot mean absence of motion, measurement, or Markov assumptions. It is qualified as freedom from a fixed linear/Gaussian family. Zero total evidence cannot be normalized directly.', 1386: 'Multivariate Gaussian density has an inverse and determinant normalization. Mean and symmetric covariance are shown, including all pose variances and cross-covariances. Covariance is a central second moment and is positive semidefinite; the ordinary full-dimensional density requires positive definiteness. Singular cases need separate support treatment. A single Gaussian is unimodal; a mixture is a different representation.', 1387: 'Particles are candidate states approximating a possibly multimodal, non-Gaussian belief. The graph places sample marks beneath a skewed curve. A blanket threshold of 100 or more particles is not a universal accuracy certificate. Finite-sample error, bias, complexity, and sampling quality are distinct; more particles cost more computation without guaranteeing lower error on every realization.', 1388: 'Linear transition x_t = F_t x_(t−1) + G_t u_t + v_t and measurement z_t = H_t x_t + w_t use zero-mean Gaussian noises with covariances V_t and W_t. The initial belief is Gaussian. The LTI introductory wording is narrower than known time-varying linear filtering. Exact Gaussian posterior and optimality statements require the usual noise-independence and correct-model assumptions.', 1389: 'All Kalman equations inspected: mean Fμ + Gu; covariance FPFᵀ + V; gain P̄Hᵀ(HP̄Hᵀ + W)⁻¹; mean plus gain times innovation; covariance (I − KH)P̄. The innovation inverse requires nonsingularity. Optimality is conditional on the model and noise assumptions.', 1390: 'Scalar prediction: initial mean 2, variance 0.25, control 2, process variance 0.5, and F = G = 1 give mean 4 and variance 0.75. The blue prior is centered at 2 and narrower; the dashed red prediction is centered at 4 and wider. Variance is not standard deviation.', 1391: 'Measurement 5 with variance 0.3 and H = 1 gives gain 5/7, posterior mean 33/7, and variance 3/14. The cyan measurement peak is at 5; the solid red posterior lies between prediction and measurement and is narrower. The source’s under-reporting slip is a specified example, not a universal slip-bias direction.', 1392: 'First-order EKF Taylor expansions were inspected. Motion Jacobians use the previous corrected state and zero noise; measurement Jacobians use the predicted state and zero noise. The printed motion evaluation x_t = μ_(t−1) has a time-index mismatch with differentiation with respect to x_(t−1).', 1393: 'EKF covariance uses F_x P F_xᵀ + F_v V F_vᵀ. Innovation covariance uses H_x P̄ H_xᵀ + H_w W H_wᵀ. The mean is propagated by nonlinear f, not just a Jacobian product. This is a local approximation, not exact arbitrary nonlinear Gaussian inference.', 1394: 'The odometry transform is T_new = T_old Trans_x(d) Rot(Δθ). Translation is along the old heading before rotation. Since the product includes the old world pose, the result is the new world pose rather than only a relative increment. The distance and orientation diagram was inspected.', 1395: 'The noisy motion uses distance noise v_d and heading noise v_θ. F_x = [[1,0,−d sinθ],[0,1,d cosθ],[0,0,1]] and F_v = [[cosθ,0],[sinθ,0],[0,1]]. Both full matrices were inspected. The old-heading translation model is not exact finite circular-arc integration during simultaneous forward motion and turning.', 1396: 'Differential-drive diagram: d is half the track width; φ is world heading. Forward speed is (v_r + v_l)/2; heading rate is (v_r − v_l)/(2d). Wheel tangential speeds are radius times angular rates. World velocities are v cosφ and v sinφ. All diagram labels and equations were inspected.', 1397: 'The full Python raster snippet was transcribed. Vt is defined from distance and converted angular standard deviations, but EKF(robot=(robot,V)) refers to undefined V in a clean namespace. RandomPath supplies control. E0 is an initial covariance. The code runs numerical and animation stages, invokes HTML without supplied notebook imports, and plots blue truth, red estimate, and green ellipses. The angular input is 0.5 degrees in code although prose omits units.', 1398: 'Odometry-only plots show separated blue ground truth and red estimate, with green positional uncertainty ellipses. The prose calls q a method; the pinned toolbox defines it as a state property. A confidence level cannot be inferred from the ellipse appearance alone. Accumulating motion uncertainty does not mean every covariance entry grows monotonically.', 1399: 'Known global landmark coordinates and unknown robot pose define localization. Range is Euclidean distance and bearing is relative to robot heading, with additive range/bearing noise. The printed single-argument arctangent loses quadrant information and fails when its denominator is zero; the explicit correction is atan2 with wrapping. Coincident robot and landmark positions make bearing and the regular Jacobian undefined. Landmark extraction is the example’s representation choice, not mandatory for every localization system.', 1400: 'The full Python raster snippet was transcribed: 20-landmark map; scalar workspace 10; odometry and sensor covariance; Unicycle and RandomPath; range limit 4 m; bearing limits −π/2 to π/2; animate=True; P0 covariance; known-map EKF; 20-second run; map and trajectory plots; ellipse call. Pinned RTB expands workspace to [−10,10] on each axis, unlike the 10×10 prose. P0 is covariance, not pose. The animate alias runs with a deprecation warning in RTB 1.4.4. The ellipse default is 95 percent in that pinned implementation.', 1401: 'A measurement and landmark ID select the appropriate known map coordinate for correction. The printed H_x has three sign errors for differentiation with respect to robot pose. The correct local matrix is [[−Δx/r,−Δy/r,0],[Δy/r²,−Δx/r²,−1]] and H_w is identity. Gain, innovation, and covariance equations were inspected. The map and trajectory overlay illustrate localization and correction, not zero-error proof.', 1402: 'Summary repeats state, belief, Bayes, Gaussian and particle representations, Kalman and extended Kalman filtering, odometry drift, and landmark fusion. Existing concepts are linked. Continuously increasing uncertainty is treated as the example’s broad trend, not a monotonicity theorem for every covariance component.', 1403: 'Two bibliographic references only.', 1404: 'Closing thank-you page, printed footer 35.'}
